WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:07.807
NARRATOR: 10 miles south of
the Great Pyramids of Giza

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:11.177
lies the Necropolis of Saqqara.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:14.481
Today, Egyptologist
Chris Naunton travels here

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:17.250
to investigate what
triggered over a thousand

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:20.820
years of pyramid building.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.357
He's been granted rare access
to explore restricted areas

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:27.293
of this necropolis.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.462
This is a pretty
exciting moment for me

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:33.600
because I've never
been inside before.

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:36.469
NARRATOR: This ancient
cemetery is home to 11

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:40.440
pyramids and hundreds of tombs.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:43.843
But one structure
dominates all others,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.781
the first pyramid ever built.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:48.982
This is where it all began.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:51.317
It is the first
monumental building

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.521
in stone anywhere in the world.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:58.525
NARRATOR: Constructed
more than 4,500 years ago,

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:02.962
this is the step pyramid tomb
of Pharaoh Djoser, a King

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:06.199
of Egypt's third dynasty.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.235
It's a revolutionary
masterpiece designed by Egypt's

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:12.772
pioneer architect, Imhotep.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:15.909
His achievement was massive
not just for the Egyptians,

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:19.112
but for humankind.

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:20.513
[grandiose music]

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.715
NARRATOR: Born as
a commoner, Imhotep

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:27.854
rose to become Pharaoh Djoser's
trusted advisor and eventually

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:31.090
his chief architect.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:34.360
He invented the
stepped pyramid using

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:38.031
stone blocks instead
of mud bricks,

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:41.868
allowing him to
build ever bigger.

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:45.338
More than 2,000 years
after Imhotep's death,

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.941
he was worshipped
as a god all the way

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.476
up to Greek and Roman times.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.781
Chris wants to
discover for himself

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:58.618
what inspired Imhotep to
design his groundbreaking step

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:00.119
pyramid.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.255
He climbs to higher
ground to examine

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:06.359
the shape of older burial
structures that surrounded.

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.361
They're called mastabas.

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:14.734
And they are these sort of
squat, square platforms,

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.506
slightly sloping,
inwardly inclining walls.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.207
NARRATOR: Chris
can make out traces

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:25.512
of these simple structures
within Imhotep's design.

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:27.747
Now that we're getting
closer to the pyramid,

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:31.751
you can really see this
series of platforms, one

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.119
on top of another.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.822
So the bottom one, in
some sense, is a mastaba.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:38.091
It's just the addition of
these successive layers

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.727
that make it into a pyramid.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:44.597
And it's an incredible
achievement, architecturally.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:52.338
NARRATOR: Built from over
500,000 tons of limestone,

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:55.842
constructed in the
mastaba-style layers,

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:59.679
the step pyramid stands
over 200 feet high,

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:03.383
then the tallest
building in the world.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:07.587
Its impact on the ancient
Egyptian landscape was huge.

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:11.324
10 more kings replicated
Imhotep's design,

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:14.193
determined to attain the
same status as the pharaoh

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.596
of the first pyramid.

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:20.033
Their tombs became some of the
most iconic sacred buildings

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:24.604
on the planet, each growing the
necropolis until it stretched

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:27.941
5 miles across the
desert to create

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.809
a sprawling city of the dead.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:35.148
Today, Imhotep's
masterpiece still

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:37.951
dominates the Egyptian desert.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:41.821
But while his structures
survive, no trace of the man

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:42.889
himself has ever been found.

